arm-intc.library/--background--               arm-intc.library/--background--

   PURPOSE
	arm-intc.library puts exec interrupt servers on the lines of the
	peripherals of the BCM2837 family (Raspberry Pi 3B, 3B+, 3A+, Zero 2
	W, CM3), for PiStorm / Emu68. It is the counterpart of gic400.library,
	which does the same on the BCM2711 (Pi 4, Pi 400, CM4).

	A line of a peripheral goes through the ARM control block of the SoC
	(brcm,bcm2836-armctrl-ic), then through the local controller of the
	cores (brcm,bcm2836-l1-intc) to the first core. Emu68 turns an
	interrupt of that core into a level 6 interrupt of the 68k
	(INTB_EXTER). The library has one server on INTB_EXTER: it finds which
	lines rang and calls their servers.

   BOARDS
	The library opens only where the interrupt parent of the root of the
	device tree is the ARM control block. Elsewhere OpenLibrary() returns
	NULL: on a BCM2711 a driver uses gic400.library.

	The library never writes the FIQ register of the ARM control block,
	nor the local controller: they belong to Emu68.

   THE NUMBERS
	A line is named by bank * 32 + line, where <bank line> is an entry of
	the "interrupts" property of a node of the device tree: 0 to 7 are the
	lines of the ARM side (bank 0), 32 to 63 the GPU lines 0 to 31 (bank
	1), 64 to 95 the GPU lines 32 to 63 (bank 2). AIC_GetInterrupt() reads
	the number from the node. <libraries/arm-intc.h> names the lines of
	bank 0, which the silicon fixes; AIC_IRQ() and AIC_IRQ_GPU() build the
	others.

   SERVERS
	A server is a struct Interrupt, called at level 6 with the registers
	of an exec interrupt server: A1 = is_Data, A5 = is_Code, A6 = SysBase,
	and D0 = the number of the line. It may change D0, D1, A0, A1, A5 and
	A6. It returns non zero, with the Z flag clear, when the interrupt was
	its own; zero, with Z set, when it was not.

	The lines are level lines: a line stays high until its device is
	cleared. A server reads the status of its device, clears it, then
	returns. A line that is still high rings again at once.

	Several servers may share a line. They are called in the order of
	ln_Pri (highest first, then in the order they were added) until one
	claims the interrupt. The first server of a line enables it, the last
	one out disables it.

	A server that the compiler writes may lose the Z flag in its epilogue
	(a MOVE that restores a register sets it again). Check the code of a C
	server, or write it in assembly.

   STORM GUARD
	A line that rings 1000 times in a row and that no server claims is
	disabled by the library, and AICI_Stormed tells it. A wrong server
	costs its own line, not the machine. AIC_EnableInt() enables the line
	again.

   WARM REBOOT
	When AmigaOS restarts and the Raspberry Pi does not, the lines that
	were enabled stay enabled. When it starts, the library disables every
	line that it finds enabled (AICI_StaleLines counts them). A driver
	clears its device before it adds its server.

   VERSIONS
	The number is the version of the resource: lib_Version.lib_Revision. A
	call that was added after the first version has its version at the end
	of its NAME line, like "(V1.5)". A change in what a call does is
	written in its FUNCTION text, "As of V1.5 ..." and "Prior to V1.5
	...". A call without a version in its NAME has been there since 1.0.

	0.1 08.11.2026 First version.

arm-intc.library/AIC_AddIntServer           arm-intc.library/AIC_AddIntServer

   NAME
	AIC_AddIntServer -- add an interrupt server to a line

   SYNOPSIS
	error = AIC_AddIntServer( irq, interrupt )
	d0                        d0   a1

	LONG AIC_AddIntServer(ULONG, struct Interrupt *);

   FUNCTION
	Adds the server to the line, by ln_Pri. When it is the first server of
	the line, the line is enabled: the server may be called before the
	function returns. The interrupt stays the caller's, and must not move
	or be freed before AIC_RemIntServer().

	Adding a server that is already on the line does nothing and returns
	AICERR_OK.

	The first server of the library puts the server of the library on the
	INTB_EXTER chain of exec, at priority 100; the last one out takes it
	off.

	Call it from a task.

   INPUTS
	irq             the number of the line (see THE NUMBERS)
	interrupt       the server: is_Code, is_Data, and ln_Pri for its place
	                on the line

   RESULTS
	error           AICERR_OK, AICERR_BADIRQ, AICERR_BADARGS (interrupt or
	                its is_Code is NULL).

   EXAMPLES
	    LONG irq = AIC_GetInterrupt(key, 0);

	    if (irq >= 0)
	    {
	        /* clear the device first: the line is enabled at once */
	        is.is_Node.ln_Type = NT_INTERRUPT;
	        is.is_Node.ln_Pri = 0;
	        is.is_Node.ln_Name = "my driver";
	        is.is_Data = mydata;
	        is.is_Code = (VOID (*)())MyServer;

	        if (AIC_AddIntServer(irq, &is) == AICERR_OK)
	            ...
	    }

   SEE ALSO
	arm-intc.library/AIC_RemIntServer(),
	arm-intc.library/AIC_GetInterrupt(),
	gic400.library/AddIntServerEx(),
	exec.library/AddIntServer()

arm-intc.library/AIC_DisableInt               arm-intc.library/AIC_DisableInt

   NAME
	AIC_DisableInt -- disable a line and keep its servers

   SYNOPSIS
	error = AIC_DisableInt( irq )
	d0                      d0

	LONG AIC_DisableInt(ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Disables the line: its servers are not called until AIC_EnableInt().
	The calls do not nest: one AIC_EnableInt() enables what any number of
	AIC_DisableInt() disabled.

	The line is disabled for every server that shares it.

	It may be called from an interrupt.

   INPUTS
	irq             the number of a line that has servers

   RESULTS
	error           AICERR_OK, AICERR_BADIRQ, AICERR_NOSERVER.

   SEE ALSO
	arm-intc.library/AIC_EnableInt()

arm-intc.library/AIC_EnableInt                 arm-intc.library/AIC_EnableInt

   NAME
	AIC_EnableInt -- enable a line again

   SYNOPSIS
	error = AIC_EnableInt( irq )
	d0                     d0

	LONG AIC_EnableInt(ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Enables a line that AIC_DisableInt() or the storm guard disabled, and
	clears AICI_Stormed. A line that is enabled stays so.

	It may be called from an interrupt.

   INPUTS
	irq             the number of a line that has servers

   RESULTS
	error           AICERR_OK, AICERR_BADIRQ, AICERR_NOSERVER (a line with
	                no server is never enabled).

   SEE ALSO
	arm-intc.library/AIC_DisableInt()

arm-intc.library/AIC_GetInterrupt           arm-intc.library/AIC_GetInterrupt

   NAME
	AIC_GetInterrupt -- read the number of a line from the device tree

   SYNOPSIS
	irq = AIC_GetInterrupt( key, index )
	d0                      a0   d0

	LONG AIC_GetInterrupt(APTR, ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Reads the entry of the "interrupts" property of the node and gives the
	number of its line. The interrupt parent of the node (the nearest
	"interrupt-parent", up the tree) must be the ARM control block of this
	library: the entry of a node of another controller is refused, it is
	not taken for a line of this one.

	The "interrupts-extended" property is not read.

   INPUTS
	key             a key of devicetree.resource, the node of the device
	index           which entry of its "interrupts" property, from 0

   RESULTS
	irq             the number of the line, 0 or more; or AICERR_BADARGS
	                (key is NULL), AICERR_NODT (no such entry),
	                AICERR_NOTOURS (another interrupt parent),
	                AICERR_BADIRQ (an entry that names no line).

   EXAMPLES
	    APTR key = DT_OpenKey("/soc/mailbox@7e00b880");
	    LONG irq = AIC_GetInterrupt(key, 0);  /* AIC_IRQ_ARM_MAILBOX */

	    DT_CloseKey(key);

   SEE ALSO
	arm-intc.library/AIC_AddIntServer(),
	devicetree.resource/DT_OpenKey()

arm-intc.library/AIC_QueryInfoTagList   arm-intc.library/AIC_QueryInfoTagList

   NAME
	AIC_QueryInfoTagList -- ask about the controller or a line

   SYNOPSIS
	count = AIC_QueryInfoTagList( irq, tagList )
	d0                            d0   a0

	ULONG AIC_QueryInfoTagList(ULONG, CONST struct TagItem *);

	count = AIC_QueryInfoTags( irq, Tag1, ... )

	ULONG AIC_QueryInfoTags(ULONG, ULONG, ...);

   FUNCTION
	Answers the questions of the tag list, about the controller when irq
	is AIC_CONTROLLER, about one line otherwise. A question about a line
	asked for the controller, or the other way round, is skipped.

	It may be called from an interrupt.

   INPUTS
	irq             AIC_CONTROLLER, or the number of a line
	tagList         the questions: each ti_Data points to the ULONG that
	                receives the answer

   TAGS
	AICI_Version    (version << 16) | revision (controller)
	AICI_ControllerAddress where the 68k sees the ARM control block
	                (controller)
	AICI_LocalAddress where the 68k sees the local controller (controller)
	AICI_GpuRouting the GPU routing register of the local controller
	                (controller)
	AICI_StaleLines lines found enabled at the start, and disabled
	                (controller)
	AICI_ServerCount the servers of every line (controller)
	AICI_Passes     level 6 interrupts in which a line of the library rang
	                (controller)
	AICI_ForeignPasses level 6 interrupts in which none did (controller)
	AICI_Enabled    TRUE when the line is enabled (line)
	AICI_Pending    TRUE when the controller shows the line pending (line)
	AICI_Servers    the servers of the line (line)
	AICI_Count      the times the line rang (line)
	AICI_Unclaimed  the times it rang and no server claimed it (line)
	AICI_Stormed    TRUE when the storm guard disabled it (line)

   RESULTS
	count           how many questions were answered; 0 when irq is not a
	                line nor AIC_CONTROLLER.

   SEE ALSO
	arm-intc.library/AIC_AddIntServer()

arm-intc.library/AIC_RemIntServer           arm-intc.library/AIC_RemIntServer

   NAME
	AIC_RemIntServer -- take an interrupt server off a line

   SYNOPSIS
	error = AIC_RemIntServer( irq, interrupt )
	d0                        d0   a1

	LONG AIC_RemIntServer(ULONG, struct Interrupt *);

   FUNCTION
	Takes the server off the line. When it was the last one, the line is
	disabled. Once the function returns, the server is not called any
	more.

	The driver quiets its device first: a line shared with other servers
	stays enabled.

	Call it from a task.

   INPUTS
	irq             the number of the line
	interrupt       a server given to AIC_AddIntServer() for this line

   RESULTS
	error           AICERR_OK, AICERR_BADIRQ, AICERR_BADARGS,
	                AICERR_NOTFOUND (it was not a server of this line).

   SEE ALSO
	arm-intc.library/AIC_AddIntServer()

